{"metadata": {"dbt_schema_version": "https://schemas.getdbt.com/dbt/run-results/v6.json", "dbt_version": "1.8.7", "generated_at": "2026-08-18T00:10:53.100668Z", "invocation_id": "4f4520b8-86f7-493e-bdab-9c018362c196", "env": {}}, "results": [{"status": "success", "timing": [{"name": "compile", "started_at": "2026-08-18T00:10:49.931854Z", "completed_at": "2026-08-18T00:10:49.964002Z"}, {"name": "execute", "started_at": "2026-08-18T00:10:49.965023Z", "completed_at": "2026-08-18T00:10:52.832646Z"}], "thread_id": "Thread-1 (worker)", "execution_time": 3.156891107559204, "adapter_response": {"_message": "SUCCESS 1", "code": "SUCCESS", "rows_affected": 1, "query_id": "01c675ea-000c-edab-0000-a0bd000a60de"}, "message": "SUCCESS 1", "failures": null, "unique_id": "model.dbt_cinemiq.int_movies_genres", "compiled": true, "compiled_code": "/*int_movie_genres.sql\n\nWHAT THIS MODEL DOES:\nTMDB genres arrive as one pipe-delimited string per movie, e.g.\n\"Action|Adventure|Sci-Fi\". That's fine for storage but useless for\nanalysis you can't GROUP BY genre when three genres live in one cell.\n\nThis model explodes that string into one row per (movie, genre) pair.\nA movie with 3 genres becomes 3 rows here. \n\nGRAIN: one row per (tmdb_id, genre).\n*/\n\nWITH movies AS (\n    SELECT\n        tmdb_id,\n        title,\n        release_year,\n        genres_raw\n    FROM CINEMAIQ.DEV_intermediate.int_movies_unified\n    WHERE genres_raw IS NOT NULL\n      AND genres_raw != ''\n),\n\nexploded AS (\n    SELECT\n        tmdb_id,\n        title,\n        release_year,\n        TRIM(value::STRING) AS genre\n    FROM movies,\n    LATERAL SPLIT_TO_TABLE(genres_raw, '|')\n)\n\nSELECT *\nFROM exploded\nWHERE genre IS NOT NULL\n  AND genre != ''", "relation_name": "CINEMAIQ.DEV_intermediate.int_movies_genres"}], "elapsed_time": 14.442595958709717, "args": {"defer": false, "populate_cache": true, "require_resource_names_without_spaces": false, "use_colors_file": true, "introspect": true, "log_f